1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,418
So far your boarding party has read its way through rooms.

2
00:00:04,318 --> 00:00:06,411
Today it walks instead.

3
00:00:08,211 --> 00:00:11,816
This is a yard, with a caretaker, a sentry and a locked door,

4
00:00:12,266 --> 00:00:15,496
and every bit of it is in four files you can read.

5
00:00:16,396 --> 00:00:17,911
You'll make it with one command,

6
00:00:18,361 --> 00:00:19,666
play it through once,

7
00:00:20,116 --> 00:00:21,942
and then take it apart.

8
00:00:23,142 --> 00:00:25,699
This half of the class gets a mission of its own.

9
00:00:26,599 --> 00:00:28,109
Same command as before,

10
00:00:28,559 --> 00:00:32,102
with a different template after the dash t: away.

11
00:00:33,902 --> 00:00:36,231
Press Enter at the question,

12
00:00:36,681 --> 00:00:38,840
and it tells you the mission is ready.

13
00:00:39,740 --> 00:00:41,534
Then the fetch line, once,

14
00:00:41,984 --> 00:00:43,741
to bring the libraries up to date.

15
00:00:44,641 --> 00:00:46,940
Both lines matter more than usual here.

16
00:00:47,390 --> 00:00:49,801
This mission has no pictures of its own.

17
00:00:50,251 --> 00:00:53,593
Its map is drawn with two art packs that missions share,

18
00:00:54,043 --> 00:00:57,847
and these two commands are what fetch them and unpack them.

19
00:00:59,647 --> 00:01:02,235
Open the folder, trust it, and run lint,

20
00:01:02,685 --> 00:01:04,231
and it comes back clean.

21
00:01:05,431 --> 00:01:07,389
A server and two consoles.

22
00:01:07,839 --> 00:01:11,012
The ship starts beside the relay, so nobody has to fly.

23
00:01:11,912 --> 00:01:14,584
Handheld, Boarding Party, beam down.

24
00:01:16,384 --> 00:01:17,547
And there's the difference:

25
00:01:17,997 --> 00:01:19,641
a map, and you on it.

26
00:01:20,541 --> 00:01:21,919
Click the ground to walk.

27
00:01:22,369 --> 00:01:24,880
Click a person, and you walk up and talk.

28
00:01:25,780 --> 00:01:29,291
This is a room, exactly like the ones you've written:

29
00:01:29,741 --> 00:01:32,202
buttons, and one of them only a medic is offered.

30
00:01:34,002 --> 00:01:35,240
He mentions a medical kit,

31
00:01:35,690 --> 00:01:39,392
and now it's on the map, where a moment ago it wasn't.

32
00:01:40,292 --> 00:01:42,293
The sentry is the hard part.

33
00:01:42,743 --> 00:01:45,010
From the weapons console I open Fire,

34
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:47,124
arm it, and click the sentry.

35
00:01:48,024 --> 00:01:49,982
It steps a cell at a time,

36
00:01:50,432 --> 00:01:53,693
so a shot often lands where it just was.

37
00:01:54,593 --> 00:01:56,909
Nothing tells you a shot missed.

38
00:01:57,359 --> 00:01:59,449
The Fire app just goes back to safe.

39
00:02:00,349 --> 00:02:02,815
Put it down, and it drops a power cell.

40
00:02:03,265 --> 00:02:05,072
The keycard opens the door,

41
00:02:05,522 --> 00:02:07,548
the cell goes into the beacon,

42
00:02:07,998 --> 00:02:09,038
and that's the game.

43
00:02:10,238 --> 00:02:11,234
Now look at the folder.

44
00:02:12,134 --> 00:02:13,628
Nine files came with it,

45
00:02:14,078 --> 00:02:16,234
and four of them are the mission.

46
00:02:18,033 --> 00:02:21,807
Two are in a folder called ground: the map itself,

47
00:02:22,257 --> 00:02:25,133
and a short list of the kinds of ground there are.

48
00:02:26,033 --> 00:02:27,695
Then the fact sheet,

49
00:02:28,145 --> 00:02:29,369
which holds everything else.

50
00:02:30,269 --> 00:02:31,519
And the story file,

51
00:02:31,969 --> 00:02:34,029
where one line loads the ground

52
00:02:34,479 --> 00:02:36,065
and one card opens the party.

53
00:02:36,965 --> 00:02:39,722
There's no Python here, and you won't write any.

54
00:02:40,922 --> 00:02:43,633
First, the yard as the crew sees it,

55
00:02:44,083 --> 00:02:47,358
on the Science console, the moment she beams down.

56
00:02:48,258 --> 00:02:50,125
The landing pad she stands on,

57
00:02:50,575 --> 00:02:52,524
and the ground she can see from it.

58
00:02:53,424 --> 00:02:55,260
The rest of the yard stays black

59
00:02:55,710 --> 00:02:57,508
until somebody in the party has seen it.

60
00:02:58,408 --> 00:03:02,056
A few steps east, and the caretaker's tent is drawn.

61
00:03:03,856 --> 00:03:07,671
Now click the map file in VS Code, and you get a picture.

62
00:03:08,121 --> 00:03:10,130
Next lecture is about that picture.

63
00:03:11,030 --> 00:03:14,148
Today press Text, and read what's underneath.

64
00:03:15,948 --> 00:03:17,324
It has three parts.

65
00:03:18,224 --> 00:03:20,984
The header says what this area is called,

66
00:03:21,434 --> 00:03:23,923
and where someone who beams down stands.

67
00:03:24,823 --> 00:03:27,571
The legend says what each character means:

68
00:03:28,021 --> 00:03:29,712
a dot is dust,

69
00:03:30,162 --> 00:03:32,906
a capital W is wall.

70
00:03:33,806 --> 00:03:36,876
And some lines carry an at sign and a name.

71
00:03:37,326 --> 00:03:38,869
That's called a mark.

72
00:03:39,319 --> 00:03:41,118
It gives a cell a name,

73
00:03:41,568 --> 00:03:43,986
so a record can say, stand here.

74
00:03:45,786 --> 00:03:48,453
Under three dashes is the map itself.

75
00:03:48,903 --> 00:03:50,147
You can find the pad,

76
00:03:50,597 --> 00:03:51,558
the tent beside it,

77
00:03:52,008 --> 00:03:53,755
and the relay house with its door.

78
00:03:54,655 --> 00:03:56,364
Notice what isn't here.

79
00:03:56,814 --> 00:04:00,156
No caretaker, no sentry, no key.

80
00:04:00,606 --> 00:04:02,367
Only the places they stand.

81
00:04:03,567 --> 00:04:04,528
Back in the game,

82
00:04:04,978 --> 00:04:07,728
the sentry walks its square south of the relay house,

83
00:04:08,178 --> 00:04:10,166
and Old Marrow sits beside his tent.

84
00:04:11,066 --> 00:04:13,658
Both of them are records in the fact sheet.

85
00:04:15,458 --> 00:04:17,473
The fact sheet has seven sections,

86
00:04:17,923 --> 00:04:20,023
and four of them you've written before.

87
00:04:20,923 --> 00:04:21,995
Three of them are new:

88
00:04:22,445 --> 00:04:24,507
props, people and hostiles.

89
00:04:26,307 --> 00:04:27,304
Here's one of the props.

90
00:04:27,754 --> 00:04:31,256
A heading, a fence, and a line of description.

91
00:04:32,156 --> 00:04:34,065
Two fields put it on the map.

92
00:04:34,515 --> 00:04:36,189
Area names the map file.

93
00:04:36,639 --> 00:04:39,050
Mark names a mark in that file's legend.

94
00:04:39,950 --> 00:04:41,774
And one field says what it does.

95
00:04:42,224 --> 00:04:44,489
Item means you can pick it up.

96
00:04:46,289 --> 00:04:48,350
A person is the same shape.

97
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:51,300
And this line is a join you already know.

98
00:04:51,750 --> 00:04:53,413
It's the key of a room.

99
00:04:54,313 --> 00:04:58,274
So a room on the ground is the room you've always written,

100
00:04:58,724 --> 00:05:01,343
with one difference: nobody walks into it.

101
00:05:01,793 --> 00:05:04,775
It belongs to the thing or the person that opens it.

102
00:05:05,975 --> 00:05:08,722
In the story file, one line loads the ground.

103
00:05:09,172 --> 00:05:11,151
You never change it.

104
00:05:11,601 --> 00:05:13,773
A new map is found by itself.

105
00:05:15,573 --> 00:05:18,585
Now remember the Boarding Party app on the handheld,

106
00:05:19,035 --> 00:05:21,296
with Kesh Relay waiting in it.

107
00:05:22,196 --> 00:05:24,948
That comes from a card at the end of the story file.

108
00:05:25,848 --> 00:05:27,980
Put it beside the one from Lecture 2.

109
00:05:28,430 --> 00:05:30,268
The first room is gone,

110
00:05:30,718 --> 00:05:33,368
because a map has no first room.

111
00:05:33,818 --> 00:05:35,039
And there's a new word:

112
00:05:35,489 --> 00:05:38,489
the area to beam down into.

113
00:05:39,389 --> 00:05:41,208
Three words here are yours.

114
00:05:41,658 --> 00:05:44,552
The signal, the name on the tile, and the area.

115
00:05:46,352 --> 00:05:47,488
Last comes the ending.

116
00:05:48,388 --> 00:05:50,774
This is the last screen of the win,

117
00:05:51,224 --> 00:05:54,478
and the sentence at the left is in the fact sheet.

118
00:05:55,378 --> 00:05:56,699
This quest carries it,

119
00:05:57,149 --> 00:05:59,756
with a second sentence beside it for a loss.

120
00:06:00,656 --> 00:06:05,145
And an answer at the beacon either completes that quest, or fails it.

121
00:06:05,595 --> 00:06:06,814
That's the whole ending.

122
00:06:08,014 --> 00:06:12,647
The long example of all this is a mission called Dawnline.

123
00:06:13,547 --> 00:06:14,911
It doesn't come with the game,

124
00:06:15,361 --> 00:06:16,380
and you don't need it.

125
00:06:18,180 --> 00:06:19,488
Look at the numbers.

126
00:06:19,938 --> 00:06:22,123
Six maps where you have one.

127
00:06:22,573 --> 00:06:25,332
Forty-two props where you have six.

128
00:06:26,232 --> 00:06:29,658
But every row is the same kind of thing as yours.

129
00:06:31,458 --> 00:06:34,499
One thing its maps have that yours doesn't:

130
00:06:34,949 --> 00:06:36,999
a way from one map to the next.

131
00:06:37,449 --> 00:06:39,607
You'll build that in two lectures' time.

132
00:06:40,507 --> 00:06:42,960
For now, change three small things,

133
00:06:43,410 --> 00:06:45,424
play each one, and put them back.

134
00:06:47,224 --> 00:06:49,394
Next time you draw a map of your own.
